# Accepted risk: vulnerabilities in the RIE binary (/usr/local/bin/aws-lambda-rie)
# embedded in public.ecr.aws/lambda/python:3.14.
#
# The Runtime Interface Emulator (RIE) is an AWS-managed Go binary used ONLY for
# local container testing (docker run). In production, AWS Lambda replaces it
# entirely with their internal runtime supervisor — the RIE binary is never loaded
# or executed. These vulnerabilities therefore have zero production attack surface.
#
# Additionally, the specific CVEs are not exploitable in our context:
#
# - The go-chi IP spoofing issues (GHSA-rjr7, GHSA-9g5q, GHSA-3fxj) relate to
#   the RealIP middleware trusting X-Forwarded-For headers. The RIE does not expose
#   any public HTTP endpoint — it only listens on localhost:8080 during local
#   development. There is no untrusted network traffic that could inject spoofed
#   headers. Even if exploited, the RIE has no IP-based access control or rate
#   limiting that could be bypassed.
#
# - CVE-2026-39824 (golang.org/x/sys integer overflow in NewNTUnicodeString) is a
#   Windows-only vulnerability affecting NTUnicodeString construction. The Lambda
#   runtime environment is Linux-based; this code path is never compiled into or
#   executed by the Linux RIE binary. CVSS score is 3.3 (Low).
#
# We cannot patch these ourselves; they require AWS to rebuild the RIE with
# updated Go dependencies (go-chi/chi v5.3.0+ and golang.org/x/sys v0.44.0+).
# Tracked upstream: https://github.com/aws/aws-lambda-runtime-interface-emulator/issues/155

# go-chi/chi v5.2.4 → v5.3.0 required
GHSA-rjr7-jggh-pgcp  # IP spoofing via X-Forwarded-For — RIE only, localhost, no IP-based ACL
GHSA-9g5q-2w5x-hmxf  # IP spoofing via X-Forwarded-For — RIE only, localhost, no IP-based ACL
GHSA-3fxj-6jh8-hvhx  # IP spoofing in middleware.RealIP — RIE only, localhost, no IP-based ACL

# golang.org/x/sys v0.21.0 → v0.44.0 required
CVE-2026-39824       # Windows-only integer overflow — Linux runtime, code path never executed
